      {
        "resource" :
          {
            "name" : "Deer Tick Surveillance: Adults (Oct to Dec) Powassan Virus Only: Beginning 2009",
            "id" : "av55-ukxh",
            "resource_name" : null,
            "parent_fxf" : [],
            "description" : "This dataset provides the results from collecting and testing adult deer ticks, also known as blacklegged ticks, or by their scientific name Ixodes scapularis. Collection and testing take place across New York State (excluding New York City) from October to December, when adult deer ticks are most commonly seen. \r\n\r\nAdult deer ticks are tested in “pools”, or groups of up to ten adult ticks per pool, for the Powassan virus, also known as Deer tick virus. These data should simply be used to educate people that there is a risk of coming in contact with ticks and tick-borne diseases.\r\n\r\nThese data only provide adult tick minimum infection rates at a precise location and at a point in time. Both measures, tick population density and minimum infection percentages, can vary greatly within a very small area and within a county. These data should not be used to broadly predict disease risk for a county.\r\n\r\nFurther below on this page you can find links to tick prevention tips, a video on how to safely remove a tick, and more datasets with tick testing results. Interactive charts and maps provide an easier way to view the data.",
            "attribution" : "health.data.ny.gov",
            "attribution_link" : null,
            "contact_email" : "no-reply@health.data.ny.gov",
